// Past-paper D — UP Assistant Professor (Pre) 2026 booklet, Physical Education section.
// HONESTY NOTES (shown to users in the app, repeated here for maintainers):
//  * Question text is transcribed from the English side of the scanned bilingual booklet. The booklet has
//    120 questions: Q1-30 general knowledge (not included) and Q31-120 Physical Education.
//  * No official answer key was supplied. The handwritten marks on a few scanned pages are one candidate's
//    marks and were NOT used. Every answer is a PROVISIONAL KEY compiled from standard textbook knowledge
//    and has not been reviewed by a subject expert.
//  * Q55 (a current-affairs item on one institute's programme as of October 2026) is left out because it
//    could not be checked against a source. Q31-Q120 otherwise are all included (89 of 90).
//  * Question numbers (paperNo) are the numbers printed on the booklet.
PYQ_PAPER_QUESTIONS.D = [];
function pqd(n, subject, topic, question, options, correct, explanation){
  PYQ_PAPER_QUESTIONS.D.push({
    id: "UPA-" + String(n).padStart(3, "0"),
    exam: ["State PSC"],
    subject, topic,
    difficulty: "Moderate",
    type: "MCQ",
    year: 2026,
    paperNo: n,
    question, options, correctIndex: correct,
    explanation,
    source: "UP Assistant Professor (Pre) 2026, Physical Education section, Q" + n + " — provisional key",
    tags: ["past-paper", "provisional-key"],
  });
}

pqd(31,"Sports Medicine","Rehabilitation modalities","Which statement most accurately defines cryotherapy without assuming a particular clinical outcome?",["Controlled mechanical elongation of periarticular tissues","Controlled removal of heat to decrease tissue temperature","Controlled electrical excitation of peripheral motor nerves","Controlled addition of heat to increase tissue temperature"],1,"Cryotherapy is the therapeutic lowering of tissue temperature by removing heat.");
pqd(32,"Fitness and Wellness","Fit India protocols","Which sequence correctly represents the age groups covered by the current MYAS Fit India fitness protocols as of October 2026? Note that the published age-group titles overlap at ages 18 and 65.",["0-4 years; 5-18 years; 19-65 years","5-17 years; 18-59 years; 60 years and above","5-18 years; 19-64 years; 65 years and above","5-18 years; 18-65 years; 65 years and above"],3,"The question states that the published titles overlap at 18 and 65, and only option D overlaps at both ages (5-18, 18-65, 65 and above). Key chosen from that wording.");
pqd(33,"Health Education","Preventive health","Which limitation of a predominantly curative, facility-based medical-care model most strongly supports the need for preventive health education?",["Its principal purpose is modifying population behaviour before illness develops rather than providing individual clinical treatment.","Its defining function is addressing environmental and social determinants without providing diagnostic or therapeutic medical services.","Its principal emphasis is community-wide disease prevention rather than the diagnosis and treatment of people who are already ill.","Its intervention commonly begins after illness develops and may devote insufficient attention to prevention and health-promoting behaviour."],3,"A curative model acts after illness appears, so it gives little attention to prevention. Options A to C describe preventive care itself, not a limitation of curative care.");
pqd(34,"Sports Management","Facilities and rules","A basketball venue reports a court as 28 metres by 15 metres, but both dimensions were measured to the outer edges of the boundary lines. Under FIBA rules, which evaluation is correct?",["It is non-compliant because the dimensions must be measured from inner edges","It is non-compliant because a FIBA court must measure 40 metres by 20 metres","It is compliant provided that every boundary line is exactly 5 centimetres wide","It is compliant because all court dimensions are measured to outer edges"],0,"FIBA court dimensions (28 m x 15 m) are measured from the inner edge of the boundary lines, so a figure taken to the outer edges does not show compliance.");
pqd(35,"Biomechanics","Newton's laws","A sprinter exerts an 800 N backward force on a starting block. Which statement correctly represents the Newton's-third-law force pair?",["Both forces act on the sprinter and therefore cancel in the sprinter's free-body diagram","The forward force begins only after the sprinter has stopped pushing the block","The block exerts a smaller forward force because some force is absorbed by the track","The block exerts an 800 N forward force on the sprinter, with the forces acting on different bodies"],3,"Action and reaction forces are equal in size, opposite in direction and act on different bodies, so they never cancel on one free-body diagram.");
pqd(36,"Sports Training","Overload and recovery","The joint consensus statement of the European College of Sport Science and the American College of Sports Medicine holds that successful training must involve overload while also avoiding the combination of excessive overload plus ______.",["Excessive recovery","Insufficient equipment variety","Inadequate recovery","Insufficient competition frequency"],2,"The overtraining consensus identifies excessive overload combined with inadequate recovery as the cause of non-functional overreaching and overtraining.");
pqd(37,"Foundations of Physical Education","Aims and objectives","A physical education department adopts holistic development through movement as its programme aim. Which objective set most appropriately operationalizes that aim?",["Higher competition win ratios, increased team selection and uniform training loads","Improved fitness scores, reduced body mass and maximal strength gains for every learner","Demonstrable motor competence, health knowledge, self-management and cooperative behaviour","Increased attendance, greater equipment use and participation in the annual sports day"],2,"Holistic development covers physical, cognitive, personal and social domains, which option C names. The others are narrow or are outputs rather than learning outcomes.");
pqd(38,"Research Methodology","Reasoning","A researcher begins with self-determination theory, derives the hypothesis that autonomy-supportive coaching predicts adherence, and then collects data to test that prediction. Which reasoning strategy is dominant?",["Abductive reasoning selecting an explanation after an anomaly","Descriptive reasoning that avoids theoretical propositions","Inductive reasoning from observations to a new theory","Deductive reasoning from theory to a testable hypothesis"],3,"Moving from a general theory to a specific testable hypothesis is deductive reasoning.");
pqd(39,"Teacher Education","NCTE","Which action falls within NCTE's statutory functions relating to surveys and planning?",["Conduct all university examinations for B.Ed. and B.P.Ed. programmes","Recommend suitable teacher-education plans to governments and universities","Appoint faculty members in every recognised teacher-education institution","Confer university degrees directly on teacher-education graduates"],1,"The NCTE Act gives the Council a role in surveys and in advising governments and universities on teacher-education plans. It does not conduct university exams, appoint faculty or award degrees.");
pqd(40,"Sports Training","Skill learning methods","A gymnast understands a complete movement sequence but repeatedly fails one transition. The teacher demonstrates the full sequence, isolates and practises the transition, and then reintegrates it into the sequence. Which method is being used?",["Progressive-part practice","Whole-part-whole practice","Pure part practice","Whole practice"],1,"Showing the whole, drilling the faulty part, then putting it back into the whole is the whole-part-whole method.");
pqd(41,"Teacher Education","NCTE","Which responsibility most directly reflects NCTE's statutory mandate?",["Regulating technical-engineering institutions and approving industrial courses","Coordinating teacher education and maintaining its norms and standards","Designing the complete school curriculum for every Indian state","Funding all central universities and allocating their research grants"],1,"NCTE's statutory mandate is the planned and coordinated development of teacher education and the maintenance of its norms and standards.");
pqd(42,"Health Education","School health","Regular posture screening among school students is considered essential mainly because it allows for ______.",["Automatic correction of deformities without any further intervention","Replacement of medical consultation with screening results alone","Early detection of spinal deviations before they progress to deformities","Complete elimination of the need for any physical education classes"],2,"Screening is for early detection so that deviations can be managed before they become fixed deformities. It does not correct them or replace clinical assessment.");
pqd(43,"Biomechanics","Angular motion","Two markers on a rigid rotating shank are 0.20 m and 0.40 m from the knee axis. At one instant, the shank's angular velocity is 5 rad·s⁻¹. Which statement is correct?",["Both markers have equal angular velocity and equal tangential speed","The proximal marker has twice the angular velocity and twice the tangential speed","The distal marker has twice the angular velocity, but both markers have equal tangential speed","Both markers have equal angular velocity, but the distal marker has twice the tangential speed"],3,"All points on a rigid body share the same angular velocity. Tangential speed v = rω, so the marker at 0.40 m moves at 2.0 m/s against 1.0 m/s for the one at 0.20 m.");
pqd(44,"Research Methodology","Methodology and methods","Which statement best distinguishes research methodology from research methods?",["Methods explain the philosophical assumptions underlying the entire investigation","Methods and methodology are always interchangeable terms with identical meanings","Methodology refers only to the instruments used to record observations","Methodology justifies the design and methods through theoretical and epistemological reasoning"],3,"Methods are the specific techniques used. Methodology is the reasoned justification for the design and methods.");
pqd(45,"Teacher Education","NCTE","In India, the statutory body responsible for regulating and recognising Bachelor of Physical Education (B.P.Ed.) programmes is ______.",["The All India Council for Technical Education (AICTE)","The National Council of Educational Research and Training (NCERT)","The University Grants Commission of India (UGC)","The National Council for Teacher Education (NCTE)"],3,"NCTE is the statutory body that regulates and recognises teacher-education programmes, including B.P.Ed.");
pqd(46,"Biomechanics","Levers and moments","During a simplified heel-raise model, body weight of 900 N acts 0.12 m from the forefoot fulcrum, while the Achilles-tendon force acts 0.18 m from it. Neglecting segment weight, which result is correct at static equilibrium?",["Mechanical advantage = 1.50; tendon force = 600 N","Mechanical advantage = 2.25; tendon force = 400 N","Mechanical advantage = 1.00; tendon force = 900 N","Mechanical advantage = 0.67; tendon force = 1,350 N"],0,"Moments balance: T x 0.18 = 900 x 0.12, so T = 600 N. Mechanical advantage = effort arm / resistance arm = 0.18 / 0.12 = 1.50.");
pqd(47,"Sports Management","Management functions","A sports federation forecasts personnel needs, recruits qualified coaches, conducts induction training and appraises employee performance. Which management function is primarily illustrated?",["Financial budgeting","Staffing","Facility management","Controlling"],1,"Manpower planning, recruitment, induction, training and appraisal make up the staffing function.");
pqd(48,"Sports Medicine","Cryotherapy","After a 20-minute crushed-ice application to the calf, skin temperature begins to rise after pack removal, but intramuscular temperature continues to fall briefly. Which explanation best accounts for this response?",["Deep muscle is cooled directly by immediate evaporative heat loss","Arterial inflow becomes permanently absent from the treated muscle","Residual tissue-temperature gradients continue conducting heat outward","Metabolic heat production stops completely when the ice is removed"],2,"After the pack is removed, deeper tissue is still warmer than the cooled superficial tissue, so heat keeps conducting outward and deep temperature continues to drop for a short time.");
pqd(49,"Sports Psychology","Group cohesion","Which four-dimensional structure belongs to the original Group Environment Questionnaire?",["Intrinsic regulation, identified regulation, external regulation and amotivation","Task orientation, ego orientation, mastery climate and performance climate","Cognitive anxiety, somatic anxiety, concentration disruption and self-confidence","Individual Attractions to Group-Task/Social and Group Integration-Task/Social"],3,"The Group Environment Questionnaire (Carron, Widmeyer and Brawley, 1985) measures cohesion through ATG-Task, ATG-Social, GI-Task and GI-Social.");
pqd(50,"Recreation","Work-leisure theories","The spillover theory of the work-leisure relationship proposes that individuals tend to choose leisure activities that ______.",["Compensate for deficiencies they experience in their work","Are chosen randomly with no reference to their occupation","Involve psychological, social and behavioural skills similar to their work","Are entirely unrelated to any aspect of their working life"],2,"Spillover theory says work experience carries over into leisure, so leisure resembles work. Compensation theory is the opposite view, and neutrality theory sees no link.");
pqd(51,"Health Education","Definition of health","The World Health Organization defines health as a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, and explicitly states that this goes beyond merely the absence of ______.",["Regular exercise","Competitive sport","Physical fitness","Disease or infirmity"],3,"WHO (1948): health is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity.");
pqd(52,"Sports Training","Training methods","The continuous training method for developing endurance involves engaging in moderate-intensity exercise for an extended period, mainly to improve ______.",["Aerobic capacity","Static flexibility of major joints","Reaction time to visual stimuli","Explosive power output"],0,"Long, steady, moderate-intensity work mainly develops aerobic capacity.");
pqd(53,"Biomechanics","Free-body diagrams","When estimating Achilles-tendon force from a static foot free-body diagram, moments are summed about the ankle joint. Why does this eliminate the unknown ankle-joint reaction from the moment equation?",["The joint-reaction line of action passes through the selected pivot","The Achilles-tendon moment arm becomes independent of joint angle","The body weight becomes equal to the joint-reaction force","The joint-reaction force becomes physically absent at equilibrium"],0,"A force whose line of action passes through the pivot has zero moment arm, so it adds nothing to the moment sum. The force is still there.");
pqd(54,"Research Methodology","Sampling","A sports federation collects the specified information from all 1,240 athletes in its defined target population. Which description is most accurate?",["It is purposive sampling because only registered athletes are eligible","It is cluster sampling because the population belongs to one federation","It is a sample survey because the resulting variables are quantitative","It is a census because every unit in the defined population is included"],3,"Collecting data from every unit of the defined population is a census, not a sample.");
pqd(56,"Recreation","Leisure and recreation","Which statement most accurately distinguishes leisure from recreation?",["Leisure is paid time away from work, whereas recreation is employer-directed fitness training.","Leisure is discretionary time away from routine obligations, whereas recreation comprises voluntarily chosen activities undertaken during that time.","Leisure is any form of physical activity, whereas recreation refers only to complete rest.","Leisure is organized competition, whereas recreation is compulsory occupational activity."],1,"Leisure is free, discretionary time. Recreation is the voluntarily chosen activity done in that time.");
pqd(57,"Sports Training","Preparation types","Which objective most specifically belongs to technical preparation rather than physical, tactical or psychological preparation?",["Improving emotional regulation and confidence under competitive pressure","Acquiring, stabilising and refining sport-specific motor skills","Developing alternative decision patterns for changing competitive situations","Increasing the physiological capacity required to sustain competition intensity"],1,"Technical preparation is about learning and refining sport-specific motor skills. A is psychological, C is tactical, D is physical.");
pqd(58,"Foundations of Physical Education","Ethics and inclusion","A physical education teacher retains the same learning outcome for all students but provides adapted equipment and additional transition time to a student with a mobility disability. Which ethical interpretation is most appropriate?",["The adaptation constitutes favouritism because disability cannot inform instructional planning","The adaptation supports equity because impartiality permits reasonable individual accommodation","The adaptation is acceptable only if the student is excluded from formal assessment","The adaptation violates impartiality because every student must receive identical treatment"],1,"Equity means giving each learner what they need to reach the same outcome. Reasonable accommodation is consistent with impartiality.");
pqd(59,"Biomechanics","Fluid mechanics","Assuming equal speed, projected area and coefficients, how does replacing air with water affect drag and buoyancy on an athlete?",["Buoyancy increases, but drag is unchanged because speed is constant","Both remain unchanged because the athlete's mass has not changed","Drag increases, but buoyancy decreases because water is less compressible","Both increase substantially because each is directly proportional to fluid density"],3,"Drag (½ρv²AC) and buoyancy (ρgV) are both proportional to fluid density, and water is about 800 times denser than air.");
pqd(60,"Statistics","Measures of central tendency","For an ordered dataset containing an even number of observations, how is the median calculated?",["By dividing the range by two","By averaging the smallest and largest observations","By selecting the most frequent observation","By averaging the two central observations"],3,"With an even count there is no single middle value, so the median is the mean of the two central values.");
pqd(61,"Foundations of Physical Education","Educational philosophies","Which curriculum emphasis is most consistent with an idealist foundation of physical education?",["Teaching movement through objective measurement, anatomical principles and sensory evidence","Enabling each learner to create personal meaning through unrestricted individual choice","Allowing natural interests to unfold through minimally directed activity in outdoor settings","Cultivating enduring values, self-discipline and mind-body harmony through purposeful movement"],3,"Idealism stresses enduring values, character and mind-body harmony. A is realism, B is existentialism, C is naturalism.");
pqd(62,"Sports Psychology","Arousal and performance","Under the traditional inverted-U interpretation of the arousal-performance relationship, performance will ______.",["Improve to a task-dependent optimum and then decline with excessive arousal","Remain constant because arousal and performance are theoretically independent","Decline continuously as arousal rises above its minimum possible level","Improve continuously as arousal rises to its maximum possible level"],0,"The inverted-U hypothesis says performance rises to an optimal arousal level (which depends on the task) and then falls.");
pqd(63,"Sports Training","Talent identification","A youth kayak programme combines anthropometric, physiological, biomechanical, technical-skill and psychological indicators. Biological maturity substantially inflates the size and power scores of some candidates. Which approach best preserves the validity of the talent-identification decision?",["Remove technical and psychological indicators from the assessment model","Replace the multidimensional model with a single coach ranking","Give raw body-size and power scores the greatest selection weight","Adjust maturity-sensitive measures and integrate all domains longitudinally"],3,"Maturity-adjusted scores combined across several domains and tracked over time reduce early-maturation bias.");
pqd(64,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Skill tests","A player obtains a high Dyer Tennis Test score but performs poorly on serve placement and tactical decision-making in matches. Which interpretation is most defensible?",["The result is invalid because the Dyer test is designed specifically for handball","The result directly establishes superior aerobic capacity during competitive tennis","The score proves proficiency in serving, rally construction and tactical decision-making","The score supports proficiency in the standardized wall-hitting task, not comprehensive tennis"],3,"The Dyer test is a standardized wall-volley test of groundstroke ability. A high score does not show serving or tactical skill.");
pqd(65,"Sports Medicine","Acute injury management","Which statement most accurately characterises the role of RICE in contemporary acute soft-tissue injury management as of October 2026?",["It requires prolonged immobilisation until pain and swelling have completely disappeared.","It establishes routine ice application as necessary for accelerating tissue regeneration in every injury.","It is a fully validated return-to-sport protocol that determines readiness for unrestricted competition.","It is a historical early-care mnemonic but does not replace assessment and progressive rehabilitation."],3,"RICE is an early-care mnemonic. Current guidance puts weight on assessment and progressive loading, and it does not treat RICE as a complete protocol.");
pqd(66,"Fitness and Wellness","Components of fitness","The health-related components of physical fitness, which a well-balanced exercise programme should address, include body composition, flexibility, muscular strength, muscular endurance and ______.",["Agility","Balance","Reaction time","Cardiorespiratory endurance"],3,"The five health-related components are cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition. Agility, balance and reaction time are skill-related.");
pqd(67,"Sports Psychology","Achievement motivation","According to the traditional achievement-motivation approach-avoidance model, which athlete is most likely to prefer a moderately difficult task with diagnostic feedback?",["An athlete who is completely indifferent to both success and failure outcomes","An athlete who selects tasks solely according to available financial rewards","An athlete whose motive to achieve success exceeds the motive to avoid failure","An athlete whose motive to avoid failure greatly exceeds the motive to succeed"],2,"In Atkinson's model, athletes high in the motive to achieve success prefer tasks of intermediate difficulty because they give the most information about ability. Those high in fear of failure avoid them.");
pqd(68,"Sports Training","Training load","Within the sports-training framework in which density represents the temporal relationship between work and recovery, which variables jointly determine training load?",["Duration, exercise mode and technical accuracy","Intensity, competition ranking and training age","Frequency, exercise order and movement complexity","Volume, intensity and density"],3,"Training load is determined by volume, intensity and density, where density is the ratio of work to recovery.");
pqd(69,"Sports Medicine","Rehabilitation","An athlete with an acute lateral ankle sprain receives cryotherapy and manual therapy for short-term symptom relief. Which accompanying rehabilitation strategy is most appropriate for restoring function and reducing recurrent instability?",["Withhold weight-bearing exercise until the athlete resumes competition","Replace structured rehabilitation with repeated cryotherapy treatments","Continue passive modalities until all exercise becomes completely painless","Progressively introduce loading, strengthening and sensorimotor exercises"],3,"Passive modalities relieve symptoms but do not restore function. Progressive loading, strengthening and sensorimotor (balance and proprioceptive) work reduce recurrent instability.");
pqd(70,"Foundations of Physical Education","Values and outcomes","Regular participation in appropriately structured physical activities can help develop ______.",["Self-discipline and cooperative behaviour","Emotional dependency and avoidance of responsibility","Performance anxiety and interpersonal distrust","Learned helplessness and social withdrawal"],0,"Well-structured physical activity is associated with positive personal and social outcomes such as self-discipline and cooperation.");
pqd(71,"Research Methodology","Research problem","A proposed study requires an unaffordable motion-capture system, access to an unavailable elite-athlete population, and completion within four weeks. Which evaluation criterion is most directly violated?",["External validity of the expected causal inference","Operational and resource feasibility of the study","Statistical conclusion validity of the final analysis","Conceptual clarity of the dependent variable"],1,"Cost, access to participants and time are feasibility constraints.");
pqd(72,"Adventure Sports and Outdoor Education","Instructional outcomes","In natural-environment adventure sports, which instructional outcome most directly demonstrates that a participant has become self-sufficient?",["Identifying hazards, evaluating uncertainty and selecting measures that reduce natural-environment risks","Maximising task difficulty while treating protective procedures as secondary to performance","Reproducing a fixed movement pattern without evaluating changing conditions","Performing a technical skill only when the instructor makes every environmental decision"],0,"Self-sufficiency means making sound risk decisions independently in changing conditions.");
pqd(73,"Sports Management","Anti-doping","In India, dope testing, doping-control education and the regulation of anti-doping rules for sportspersons are primarily carried out by the ______.",["Board of Control for Cricket (BCCI)","National Anti-Doping Agency (NADA)","Indian Olympic Association (IOA)","Sports Authority of India (SAI)"],1,"NADA India is the national anti-doping organisation responsible for testing, education and rule implementation.");
pqd(74,"Teaching Methods","Spectrum of teaching styles","Which transition crosses Mosston and Ashworth's discovery threshold from the reproduction cluster to the production cluster?",["Inclusion Style - E to Guided Discovery Style - F","Convergent Discovery Style - G to Divergent Discovery Style - H","Reciprocal Style - C to Self-Check Style - D","Command Style - A to Practice Style - B"],0,"Styles A to E (Command to Inclusion) form the reproduction cluster. The discovery threshold lies between E and F, where Guided Discovery begins the production cluster.");
pqd(75,"Foundations of Physical Education","NEP 2020","Which classroom practice best exemplifies sports-integrated learning as envisaged in NEP 2020?",["Using an indigenous team game to teach measurement, cooperation and responsibility","Replacing subject learning with unstructured recreation throughout the school day","Restricting all games to optional after-school competition for selected athletes","Removing physical activity so that additional written tests can be conducted"],0,"NEP 2020 promotes sports-integrated pedagogy, using games (including indigenous ones) to teach subject content and values.");
pqd(76,"Sports Training","Tactical preparation","Research on tactical preparation in sport games indicates that when a player's physical fitness is inadequately developed, the range of tactical options available to that player is generally ______.",["Completely unaffected by the player's level of fitness","Determined solely by the opposing team's fitness level","Reduced, even when tactical preparation itself is of high quality","Increased, regardless of the player's level of fitness"],2,"Tactical execution depends on physical capacity, so poor fitness narrows the options even when tactical preparation is good.");
pqd(77,"Statistics","ANOVA","In a one-way ANOVA, SS between = 120 with df between = 3, and SS within = 320 with df within = 32. What is the F statistic?",["4.00","12.00","3.00","2.00"],0,"MS between = 120 / 3 = 40 and MS within = 320 / 32 = 10, so F = 40 / 10 = 4.00.");
pqd(78,"Foundations of Physical Education","Educational philosophies","Which physical education programme most coherently applies a naturalist educational philosophy?",["Learners master objectively analysed techniques through systematic demonstration, measurement and correction.","Learners freely select activities to construct personal meaning and assume responsibility for their choices.","Learners reproduce ideal movement forms selected to cultivate universal moral truths under strong teacher direction.","Learners explore developmentally appropriate movement tasks in natural settings with limited teacher intervention."],3,"Naturalism stresses natural development, natural settings and minimal teacher interference. A is realism, B is existentialism, C is idealism.");
pqd(79,"Sports Training","Skill learning methods","Which skill profile most strongly favours whole practice rather than part practice?",["Many separable components with minimal temporal interaction","Independent subroutines that can be practised without altering coordination","High complexity with largely independent movement components","Low complexity with highly interdependent movement components"],3,"Whole practice suits skills of low complexity whose parts are highly interdependent (high organisation). Complex skills with independent parts suit part practice.");
pqd(80,"Sports Psychology","Personality","A personality inventory indicates that a physical education teacher scores low on extraversion. Which use of this result is most professionally defensible?",["Treat the score as a permanent diagnosis overriding observed classroom performance.","Publish the result so that students can decide whether to attend the teacher's classes.","Use it as one source of information when tailoring voluntary professional support.","Remove the teacher because low extraversion proves teaching incompetence."],2,"A trait score is one piece of information. It is not a diagnosis, should stay confidential and does not predict competence.");
pqd(81,"Statistics","Type I error","Two hundred independent tests are conducted at α = 0.05, and every null hypothesis is actually true. Approximately how many Type I errors are expected?",["20","190","10","5"],2,"Expected false positives = 200 x 0.05 = 10.");
pqd(82,"Anatomy and Physiology","Gait","A single gait cycle in human walking is divided into two major phases, known as the stance phase and the ______.",["Rebound phase","Contact phase","Swing phase","Recovery phase"],2,"The gait cycle has a stance phase (foot on the ground, about 60 percent) and a swing phase (foot in the air, about 40 percent).");
pqd(83,"Sports Management","Competition structures","A university conducts a tournament at a municipal stadium, but participation is restricted to teams representing departments of that university. How should the tournament be classified?",["International, because a public stadium is being used","Professional, because the tournament uses an external facility","Extramural, because the venue is located outside the university campus","Intramural, because all participants belong to the same institution"],3,"Intramural means within the walls of one institution. The classification depends on who participates, not on where the venue is.");
pqd(84,"Yoga","International Day of Yoga","Which statement accurately describes the establishment of the International Day of Yoga?",["The International Olympic Committee designated 21 June through the Olympic Charter","UN General Assembly Resolution 69/131 proclaimed 21 June as the annual observance","A World Health Organization treaty made yoga compulsory on 21 June","UNESCO Resolution 70/1 designated 11 June as the observance in 2015"],1,"The UN General Assembly adopted resolution 69/131 in December 2014, proclaiming 21 June as the International Day of Yoga.");
pqd(85,"Research Methodology","Historical research","Before using an unsigned 1932 training diary as historical evidence, a researcher examines its provenance, paper, ink, and consistency with the claimed date. This procedure primarily constitutes ______.",["Statistical triangulation estimating sampling error","Experimental control manipulating an independent variable","Internal criticism assessing the author's credibility","External criticism assessing the source's authenticity"],3,"External criticism asks whether the document is genuine (provenance, paper, ink, date). Internal criticism asks whether its content and author are credible.");
pqd(86,"Sports Medicine","First aid","In providing first aid for a suspected fracture, after checking the casualty's condition and controlling any bleeding, a key subsequent step is ______.",["Encouraging the casualty to walk on the injured limb","Immobilisation of the affected part to prevent further damage","Immediate forceful realignment of the fractured bone","Vigorous massage of the fractured limb to relieve pain"],1,"The affected part is immobilised (splinted) to prevent further injury before transport.");
pqd(87,"Anatomy and Physiology","Joints","A synovial joint, which is the most common and freely moveable type of joint in the body, is functionally classified as a ______.",["Amphiarthrosis","Symphysis","Synarthrosis","Diarthrosis"],3,"Functionally, freely movable joints are diarthroses, and synovial joints are the standard example.");
pqd(88,"Foundations of Physical Education","Theories of play","Which situation is most directly predicted by the classical recreation or relaxation theory of play?",["A mentally fatigued worker chooses contrasting physical play to restore depleted capacity","An athlete displays aggressive play solely to release an unconscious hostile drive","A young animal rehearses hunting actions needed for adult survival","A child reenacts ancestral behaviour because development repeats evolutionary history"],0,"The recreation or relaxation theory says play restores energy depleted by work. B is the catharsis theory, C is Groos's pre-exercise theory, D is the recapitulation theory.");
pqd(89,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Skill tests","What conclusion is most appropriate when a player obtains a high score on the McDonald Soccer Wall-Volley Test?",["The player has established maximal aerobic power without physiological testing","The player has demonstrated standardized overarm passing ability in handball","The player has demonstrated superior tactical decisions in every match situation","The player performed well on the standardized repeated soccer wall-volley task"],3,"A skill test measures the standardized task it sets. It does not show aerobic power or tactical decisions in matches.");
pqd(90,"Health Education","Workplace health","Workplace physical activity programmes have been found to generate economic benefits for employers mainly through favourable changes in productivity, absenteeism, and ______.",["The exchange rate between international currencies","The interest rates set by central banks","Employee turnover and workplace injury rates","The global price of agricultural commodities"],2,"The business case for workplace activity programmes cites productivity, absenteeism, turnover and injury or health-cost outcomes. The other options are macroeconomic variables outside the employer's control.");
pqd(91,"Sports Psychology","Motivation","A team captain seeks influence primarily to coordinate teammates, allocate responsibilities and achieve collective goals rather than to gain personal status. In McClelland's framework, this most strongly represents ______.",["Need for achievement limited to individual performance standards","Failure-avoidance motivation directed toward reducing evaluation","Socialised need for power directed toward collective objectives","Need for affiliation expressed through avoidance of difficult decisions"],2,"McClelland distinguishes personalised power (for status) from socialised power (influence used for group goals).");
pqd(92,"Health Education","School health","Which option is not one of the four interrelated components of the Comprehensive School Health framework?",["Partnerships and services","Competitive interschool sport selection","Teaching and learning","Social and physical environment"],1,"The four pillars are teaching and learning, social and physical environment, healthy school policy, and partnerships and services. Interschool sport selection is not one.");
pqd(93,"Motor Development","Maturation and experience","Which statement best represents the relationship between biological maturation and experience in motor development?",["Environmental opportunities affect motivation but have no influence on emerging movement patterns.","Motor skills emerge through interactions among maturation, individual characteristics, task demands and environmental experience.","Training can produce any motor skill even when the necessary neuromuscular capacities are absent.","Maturation alone produces motor skills in a fixed form, regardless of practice or environment."],1,"Current views (for example Newell's constraints model) treat motor development as an interaction of the individual, the task and the environment.");
pqd(94,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Basic terms","In test, measurement and evaluation, a test is best defined as a specific instrument or procedure used by an administrator mainly to ______.",["Replace the need for any further teaching","Determine the final budget of a sports programme","Permanently improve the physical fitness of an athlete","Elicit a response from the person being tested"],3,"A test is the tool or procedure that elicits a response, which is then measured.");
pqd(95,"Sports History","Indian participation in the Olympics","At which Olympic Games did Indian women first compete in athletics (track and field), represented by Nilima Ghose and Mary D'Souza?",["Melbourne 1956","Rome 1960","London 1948","Helsinki 1952"],3,"Nilima Ghose and Mary D'Souza were India's first women Olympic athletes, at Helsinki 1952.");
pqd(96,"Teaching Methods","Class observation","A supervisor wants to determine whether a teacher's questions and feedback are distributed equitably across pupils seated in different areas. Which observation technique is most appropriate?",["A global rating assigned from the supervisor's overall impression","An equipment inventory completed after the observed lesson","Selective verbatim recording without identifying the participating pupils","Seating-chart coding of each pupil's location and recorded interaction"],3,"Seating-chart coding links each interaction to a pupil and a location, so distribution can be checked.");
pqd(97,"Sports Training","Periodisation","In the periodisation of a training plan, the annual or long-term training period is generally known as the ______.",["Macrocycle","Nanocycle","Microcycle","Mesocycle"],0,"Macrocycle is the long-term (usually annual) cycle. Mesocycles are medium blocks and microcycles are about a week.");
pqd(98,"Yoga","Yoga and heritage","UNESCO's inscription of yoga on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity supports which interpretation of physical culture as heritage?",["Yoga is a fixed archaeological monument preserved at one ancient geographical site.","Yoga is solely a biomedical exercise method without mental or spiritual dimensions.","Yoga is a living practice integrating postures, meditation, controlled breathing and other techniques transmitted through traditional and modern settings.","Yoga is exclusively a competitive sport whose heritage depends on uniform international scoring."],2,"Intangible cultural heritage is living practice transmitted across generations, which describes yoga.");
pqd(99,"Sports Management","Sport policy","Which sport-policy intervention most directly operationalises the Kazan Action Plan's contribution to SDG 5?",["Expanding transport infrastructure surrounding major stadiums","Providing nutrition programmes without addressing gender access","Removing participation barriers and increasing women's leadership in sport","Reducing venue emissions through renewable-energy procurement"],2,"SDG 5 is gender equality, so the intervention that removes barriers and builds women's leadership in sport fits it.");
pqd(100,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Test selection criteria","A laboratory test is valid and reliable but requires an unavailable metabolic cart and specialist staff. A validated field test can be administered safely with existing resources. Which selection criterion most directly favours the field test?",["Availability of normative data","Administrative feasibility and practicability","Inter-rater objectivity","Content validity"],1,"Both tests are valid and reliable, so the deciding factor is practicability: equipment, staff and cost.");
pqd(101,"Biomechanics","Electromyography","Which conclusion is most defensible when interpreting normalised surface EMG recorded during sprinting?",["Equal EMG amplitudes prove equal force despite different contraction conditions","EMG characterises activation timing and amplitude but is not a direct force measurement","EMG timing directly determines joint torque without inverse-dynamics analysis","EMG amplitude provides an exact direct measure of individual-muscle force"],1,"Surface EMG shows when and how strongly a muscle is activated. Force also depends on length, velocity and other factors, so EMG is not a direct force measure.");
pqd(102,"Biomechanics","Sports equipment","A shoe combining highly resilient foam, a curved plate and rocker geometry improves running economy relative to a conventional shoe. Which causal conclusion is most defensible?",["Resilient foam alone caused the improvement because longitudinal bending stiffness is always irrelevant","The improvement proves that every runner will respond equally to every shoe using the same plate material","The plate alone caused the entire improvement because the other shoe features cannot influence economy","The integrated design improved economy, but a component-controlled study is needed to isolate the plate's effect"],3,"Several features changed at once, so the benefit belongs to the whole design. Isolating one component needs a study that controls the others.");
pqd(103,"Sports History","Ancient Olympic Games","Which statement most accurately reflects the historical evidence concerning the end of the ancient Olympic festival?",["Their suppression is traditionally linked to Theodosius I around 393 CE, although evidence indicates that some athletic festivals continued into the early fifth century.","They ended only when Justinian closed the philosophical schools in 529 CE, with no earlier restrictions on pagan festivals.","The Games were transferred to Rome under Augustus and continued there without alteration for several centuries.","The Games ended immediately after the Roman conquest of Greece in 146 BCE, with no later Greek athletic festivals."],0,"Tradition links the end to Theodosius I (about 393 CE), but the evidence is more gradual and some festivals continued into the early fifth century.");
pqd(104,"Sports Management","ICT in sport","Which scenario most completely demonstrates the use of ICT in sport-performance support?",["A teacher writes attendance on paper and stores it in a classroom cupboard","Wearable data are captured, processed, stored and shared digitally with the coach","An athlete receives oral instructions without data collection or digital processing","A coach observes practice without recording or communicating any information"],1,"ICT use covers capturing, processing, storing and communicating data digitally. Only B does all four.");
pqd(105,"Sports Psychology","Personality","Which interpretation of a high neuroticism score is most defensible within Eysenck's personality framework?",["It demonstrates superior tolerance of every form of competitive stress","It predicts athletic performance independently of task and environment","It indicates greater proneness to emotional instability and negative affect","It proves the presence of a permanent clinical anxiety disorder"],2,"In Eysenck's model, high neuroticism is a trait showing proneness to emotional instability and negative emotion. It is not a diagnosis.");
pqd(106,"Research Methodology","Research problem","Which feature most directly demonstrates that a clearly stated research problem is empirically researchable?",["Its proposed analysis is guaranteed to produce statistical significance","Its accessible sample is identical to the entire target population","Every variable can be experimentally manipulated by the researcher","Its principal variables can be operationally defined and observed"],3,"A problem is researchable when its main variables can be defined operationally and observed or measured.");
pqd(107,"Health Education","Physical activity and young people","Regular participation in physical activity among children and young people is described as providing psychosocial benefits, including confidence, relief of tension, achievement and ______.",["Social interaction and integration","Complete elimination of all future health risk","Guaranteed selection for national sports teams","Permanent immunity to all infectious disease"],0,"The other options are absolute claims. Social interaction and integration is a recognised psychosocial benefit.");
pqd(108,"Sports Medicine","Massage","A therapist begins massage with continuous strokes that maintain hand contact and glide longitudinally over a broad tissue area. Which manipulation is being performed?",["Tapotement using rhythmic percussive strokes","Friction using localised transverse pressure","Petrissage using lifting and kneading strokes","Effleurage using continuous gliding strokes"],3,"Effleurage consists of continuous gliding strokes with constant hand contact, usually used to begin and end a massage.");
pqd(109,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Leger shuttle run","A 16-year-old reaches a final speed of 12.0 km/h in the Leger 20 m shuttle run. Using VO2max = 31.025 + 3.238S - 3.248A + 0.1536SA, where S is the final running speed (km/h) and A is age (years), what is the estimated VO2max?",["47.4 mL/kg/min","52.0 mL/kg/min","44.8 mL/kg/min","41.2 mL/kg/min"],0,"31.025 + 3.238(12) - 3.248(16) + 0.1536(12)(16) = 31.025 + 38.856 - 51.968 + 29.491 = 47.4 mL/kg/min.");
pqd(110,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Reliability and objectivity","A movement-quality test produces an inter-rater ICC of 0.96 but a two-week test-retest coefficient of 0.62. Which interpretation is most accurate?",["Both scorer objectivity and temporal reliability are demonstrated as perfect","Neither coefficient provides information about consistency of test scores","Temporal reliability is high, but scorer objectivity is necessarily poor","Scorer objectivity is high, but temporal score reliability is only moderate"],3,"The inter-rater ICC (0.96) shows scorers agree closely. The test-retest value (0.62) shows only moderate stability over two weeks.");
pqd(111,"Sports Psychology","Exercise and sport psychology","Which pairing most accurately distinguishes the characteristic emphases of exercise psychology and sport psychology?",["Exercise psychology - stadium behaviour; sport psychology - clinical pharmacology","Exercise psychology - physical-activity participation; sport psychology - competitive performance","Exercise psychology - sport contracts; sport psychology - exercise biochemistry","Exercise psychology - elite tactics; sport psychology - equipment design"],1,"Exercise psychology focuses on participation, adherence and health benefits of activity. Sport psychology focuses on performance in competitive sport.");
pqd(112,"Biomechanics","Sports equipment","As of October 2026, which conclusion about increasing running-shoe midsole thickness is best supported by current biomechanical evidence?",["It has no effect on sensory feedback, joint mechanics or impact-related variables","It permanently increases VO2max independently of training status and running speed","It necessarily prevents running injury because lower perceived impact proves lower tissue loading","It may improve cushioning but alter plantar sensation; injury risk cannot be inferred from thickness alone"],3,"Thicker midsoles change cushioning and foot-sole sensation, but the link to injury risk is not established. Options A to C make absolute claims.");
pqd(113,"Yoga","Ashtanga yoga","In Patanjali's classical ashtanga-yoga system, which sequence correctly orders the eight limbs from ethical disciplines to meditative absorption?",["Asana, yama, niyama, pratyahara, pranayama, samadhi, dharana, dhyana","Yama, asana, niyama, dharana, pratyahara, pranayama, samadhi, dhyana","Niyama, yama, pranayama, asana, dhyana, pratyahara, dharana, samadhi","Yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi"],3,"The order is yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi.");
pqd(114,"Sports Management","Anti-doping","Which statement most accurately distinguishes the World Anti-Doping Code's definition of doping from its ethical rationale?",["Doping means using any supplement, whereas the spirit of sport determines liability","Doping is the occurrence of one or more anti-doping rule violations; the spirit of sport supports their prohibition","Doping means only a positive laboratory test, whereas health risk determines intent","Doping means any performance improvement, whereas fairness determines the sanction"],1,"The Code defines doping as the occurrence of one or more anti-doping rule violations. The spirit of sport is the ethical basis for prohibiting them.");
pqd(115,"Sports Training","Talent identification","A youth talent programme uses a single cross-sectional assessment. An early-maturing athlete scores highly in body size and strength but only moderately in technical, perceptual and psychological measures. Which policy would best reduce maturity-related selection bias?",["Exclude all physical and anthropometric measures from the selection process","Postpone every form of talent assessment until athletes reach adulthood","Use maturity-adjusted multidimensional scores with repeated longitudinal assessment","Select athletes primarily from their current anthropometric and strength scores"],2,"Maturity-adjusted scores across several domains, assessed repeatedly, reduce the advantage of early maturers.");
pqd(116,"Foundations of Physical Education","Theories of play","Which proposition uniquely distinguishes Groos's pre-exercise theory from surplus-energy, recreation and recapitulation theories of play?",["Play restores capacities depleted by work through a change of activity","Play repeats successive cultural stages from ancestral human development","Play discharges energy remaining after immediate biological needs are met","Play rehearses incompletely developed behaviours required in adult life"],3,"Groos saw play as practice for adult life. A is the recreation theory, B is recapitulation, C is surplus energy.");
pqd(117,"Test, Measurement & Evaluation","Reliability","A researcher intends to measure stable typical performance across three exchangeable trials. Assuming independent random error and no systematic learning or fatigue, which scoring rule is most likely to improve reliability?",["Record only the first completed trial","Select the highest trial regardless of variability","Calculate the arithmetic mean of the three trials","Record only the lowest trial score"],2,"Averaging independent trials cancels random error and raises reliability. Single-trial or best-trial scores keep or inflate the error.");
pqd(118,"Anatomy and Physiology","Neuromuscular junction","Which neurotransmitter-receptor pairing mediates normal transmission from a somatic alpha motor neuron to an adult skeletal muscle fibre?",["Glutamate acting on NMDA-type glutamate receptors","Acetylcholine acting on nicotinic muscle-type receptors","Noradrenaline acting on beta-1 adrenergic receptors","Acetylcholine acting on muscarinic M2 receptors"],1,"The neuromuscular junction uses acetylcholine acting on nicotinic (muscle-type) receptors.");
pqd(119,"Sports Management","Sports public relations","Which competency profile is most directly relevant to a sports public-relations role?",["Clinical diagnosis, surgical procedures, pharmacology and inpatient case management","Crop breeding, irrigation engineering, soil chemistry and livestock management","Structural design, soil testing, load calculations and reinforced-concrete detailing","Media writing, stakeholder engagement, crisis communication and digital-media monitoring"],3,"Public relations work centres on media writing, stakeholder engagement, crisis communication and monitoring.");
pqd(120,"Health Education","Principles of health education","A fundamental guiding principle of health education, commonly summarised as \"prevention is better than cure\", emphasises that health care should primarily be ______.",["Delivered only after a disease has spread","Preventive and accessible to all people","Reserved exclusively for those already ill","Curative and limited to hospitals only"],1,"The principle puts prevention first and treats health care as something accessible to everyone.");
